                <pb facs="0340" n="303" xml:id="pg303"/>

                <head>105. Memorandum From the Director of the Office of Greek, Turkish, and Iranian
                    Affairs (Richards) to the Assistant
                    Secretary of State for Near Eastern, South Asian, and African Affairs (Byroade)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d105-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1952-07-30"
                        >July 30, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">Suggestions Regarding Certain CIA Actions Concerning Iran</p>
                <p>During a conversation between Mr. Kermit
                        Roosevelt, [<hi rendition="#italic">name not declassified</hi>],
                    Mr. Jernegan and officers of GTI, certain suggestions were made regarding
                    aspects of CIA action to prepare for possible
                    developments in Iran.</p>
                <p>1. In case of chaos or civil war in Iran, the United States might desire to
                    support armed action by “friendly” tribal, army or government elements. To
                    prepare for such possible action CIA will need
                    to stockpile weapons and ammunition near but not in Iran. In view of the ominous
                    course of events in Iran it seems desirable that such stockpiling be commenced
                    urgently and a policy decision on this matter is desired.</p>
                <p>2. [<hi rendition="#italic">2½ lines not declassified</hi>] It would probably be
                    useful for CIA to respond favorably to this
                    offer but policy authorization from the State Department must be obtained.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
